      "id": 1,
      "claim": "In a depth‑first search of a connected undirected graph, every edge that is not in the DFS tree connects a vertex to one of its ancestors in the DFS tree (i.e., it is a back edge).",
      "evidence": "When DFS explores an undirected edge (x,y) and y has already been visited, the recursive call that discovered x must have been made from y or one of y’s descendants; therefore y lies on the recursion stack and is an ancestor of x. Undirected graphs have no cross edges in DFS.",
      "weight": 0.4,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "For a non‑tree edge (u,v) with pre(u) < pre(v), u is the ancestor and v the descendant in T.",
      "evidence": "By step 1 one endpoint is an ancestor of the other. In a preorder traversal ancestors are visited before descendants, so the endpoint with the smaller preorder number must be the ancestor.",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "If u is an ancestor of v, then the lowest common ancestor of u and v is u.",
      "evidence": "The LCA is defined as the deepest vertex that is an ancestor of both vertices. Since u is itself an ancestor of v, no descendant of u can be an ancestor of u, so u is the deepest common ancestor.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "For an ancestor u and descendant v, post(v) < post(u).",
      "evidence": "In a postorder traversal a vertex is recorded after all vertices in its subtree have been recorded; therefore every descendant finishes before its ancestor.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Statement I (post(u) < post(v)) is false, while statements II and III are true for every such edge.",
      "evidence": "From step 4 we have post(v) < post(u), contradicting I. Steps 2 and 3 give that II and III hold universally.",
